---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LINKS, EMAIL, AT_RELEASE } from '../data/six10nine';
---

<Base title="Contribute" description="Bug reports, ideas and pull requests to six10nine, and the contributor agreement they need." docs>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1>Contribute</h1>
  <p class="lede">Bug reports, ideas and pull requests are welcome.</p>
  <p>
    The repository, <span class="mono">{LINKS.repo}</span>, goes public at release. <span class="badge">{AT_RELEASE}</span>
  </p>

  <h2>Sign the CLA</h2>
  <p>
    Elliott Wave LLC owns all of six10nine’s intellectual property and may also license it commercially, so every
    contributor signs the Contributor Copyright Assignment Agreement once. The agreement is under legal review and may
    change before the first outside contribution is merged.
  </p>
  <p>In plain words: you assign the copyright in your contribution to Elliott Wave LLC, and you get a permanent licence
    back to use your own contribution for any purpose. Everyone else receives it under the project’s PolyForm
    Noncommercial licence.</p>
  <p>To sign, put this line in your first pull request’s description:</p>
  <pre><code>I have read the Copyright Assignment Agreement and I hereby sign it</code></pre>
  <p>
    Pull requests without it can’t be merged. Contributing for your employer? Make sure you’re allowed to, or have them
    contact <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL.licensing}`}>{EMAIL.licensing}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you start</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Talk first about big changes.</strong> Open an issue before a new feature, a change to the game side, or
      anything touching the Einstein@home integration. Bug fixes and small improvements can go straight to a pull request.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Security issues</strong> go to <a href="/security">security@elliottwave.studio</a>, never to public issues.</li>
    <li><strong>Read <code>docs/ARCHITECTURE.md</code>.</strong> It’s the design of record; update it in the same pull request if you change behaviour it describes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Build and test</h2>
  <p>macOS 14 or later and Xcode 26 (Swift 6.2).</p>
  <pre><code>cd Packages/Six10NineKit
swift build
swift test                 # fast, no permissions needed
cd ../..
scripts/install-app.sh     # build, sign with your Apple Development certificate, install, launch</code></pre>
  <p>
    Sign with a stable Apple Development identity: macOS ties the Accessibility permission to the signature, and ad-hoc
    builds lose it on every reinstall.
  </p>

  <h2>A good pull request</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Logic lives in <code>Six10NineKit</code>, with tests.</strong> Anything that makes a decision belongs in the library as plain value types with XCTest coverage. The app target only wires macOS events to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Never get in the way.</strong> Keys six10nine doesn’t use must reach apps untouched. Overlays never take focus. Nothing polls.</li>
    <li><strong>Private by default.</strong> No analytics, no new network calls, and AI stays on-device.</li>
    <li><strong>Match the surrounding code.</strong> Swift 6 strict concurrency, short comments that explain why, no new dependencies without discussion.</li>
    <li><code>swift test</code> passes, and the pull request says what you checked by hand: the event tap, menus and overlays aren’t covered by automated tests.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Please follow the Code of Conduct in the repository.</p>
</Base>
